  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The empirical evidence of leverage is derived from a single case study of one Chinese SOE in a Greek port, which may not fully represent how other state-owned enterprises operate in different national contexts.",
      "what": "This study establishes an analytical framework to evaluate how home countries gain geopolitical leverage through state-owned enterprise (SOE) investments in foreign shipping, terminal operations, and port management. The framework is applied to a case study of the Chinese SOE COSCO and its operations in the Port of Piraeus, Greece, to determine the conditions under which these maritime investments translate into political influence.",
      "why": "It provides a structured method to assess the geopolitical risks of foreign state-backed infrastructure investments, demonstrating empirically that COSCO's presence in Piraeus yields tangible leverage for China over Greece."
    }
